WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.523
- Hello, and welcome everybody.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.933
Thank you all for joining
us, thank you for coming,

00:00:19.028 --> 00:00:24.028
and welcome to How It's
Made, the RITM book edition,

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.040
an event in which we'll celebrate

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.560
and hear from three Yale faculty members

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.610
who have authored important
books in recent months.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.190
I'm Steven Petty, director of Yale Center

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.840
for the Study of Race, Indigeneity,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.610
and Transnational Migration,

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.810
and professor of History, American Studies

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.910
and Ethnicity, Race, and Migration.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.840
I'm gonna be moderating
today's conversation

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.160
which should run a little
longer than an hour.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.670
Since its founding in 2016,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.340
the RTTM Center has worked
to support publications

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.930
by students and faculty

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.600
and to organize events of
interest on the Yale campus

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.120
and other academic settings

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.680
and for wider groups of
people who aren't affiliated

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.790
with a college or a university.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:06.950
So I hope that you'll keep
your eyes open for news

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.410
of other events and opportunities

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.830
through our RITM in the
days and weeks to come.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.470
If you aren't already receiving
our weekly newsletter,

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:15.930
please sign up for it today.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.976
The link will be posted soon in the chat.

00:01:18.976 --> 00:01:21.690
I'm gonna introduce
briefly our three speakers

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.080
in the order in which
there'll be discussing

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.580
their books today.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.320
Each of them will talk
for five to 10 minutes,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.290
and then we'll open
the floor to questions.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.690
So if you have a question for
one or more of our authors,

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.359
please post it in the Q&amp;A.

00:01:36.359 --> 00:01:40.190
We are very eager to hear from you,

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.570
and I know that our authors
would love to respond

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.560
to any questions that you have.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.750
Our first speaker today
is Laura Barraclough,

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.020
the Sarai Ribicoff Associate Professor

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.990
of American Studies at Yale.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.560
Laura is an interdisciplinary
scholar of land,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.440
memory, identity, and
inequality in the United States.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.090
She's the author of Making
the San Fernando Valley:

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.640
Rural Landscapes, Urban
Development , and White Privilege,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.720
and she's co-author with
Laura Pulido and Wendy Cheng

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.290
of A People's Guide to Los Angeles.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.840
She will be speaking
today about her new book,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.560
Charros: How Mexican
Cowboys are Remapping Race

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.160
and American Identity,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.121
published by the University
of California press.

00:02:22.121 --> 00:02:25.322
Daniel Martinez HoSang
is an Associate Professor

00:02:25.322 --> 00:02:28.940
of Ethnicity, Race and
Migration in American Studies

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.180
with a secondary appointment

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.540
in the Department of Political
Science at Yale University.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.100
Dan's work explores US
political culture in many sites,

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:40.140
including electoral,
politics, social movements,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.970
and cultural formations.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.110
He is the author of Racial Propositions:

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:48.130
Ballot Initiatives and the
Making of Postwar California,

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:51.480
and the co-editor of three
books, including one entitled,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.460
Seeing Race Again:
Countering Colorblindness

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.330
Across the Disciplines.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.900
Dan will be speaking today
about his recent book,

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.280
Producers, Parasites, Patriots:

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.950
Race, and the New Right
Wing politics of Precarity,

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.890
which he co-authored with Joseph Lowndes

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.794
and which was published

00:03:08.794 --> 00:03:11.170
by the University of Minnesota press.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.810
Our third speaker today
is Ana Ramos-Zayas,

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.970
a professor of Ethnicity,
Race and Migration,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:20.090
and of American Studies and
Anthropology at Yale University.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.320
Ana is the author of
National Performances:

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.230
Class, Race and Space
in Puerto Rican Chicago,

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.660
and of Street Therapists: Affect, Race

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.400
and Neoliberal Personhood
in Latino Newark.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.939
She is also coauthor of Latino Crossings,

00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:37.870
Mexicans, Puerto Ricans and the Politics

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.150
of Race and Citizenship,

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.195
and she has two co-edited
books that are forthcoming,

00:03:42.195 --> 00:03:45.370
(speaking in foreign language)
and Latino-Latina Studies,

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.440
and Whiteness in Latin
America and the Caribbean.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.080
Ana will be speaking today
about her recent book,

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.520
entitled, Parenting
Empires: Class Whiteness,

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.680
and the Moral Economy of
Privilege in Latin America,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.920
which was recently published
by Duke University Press.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.750
And with that, I'm gonna
turn this over to Laura

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.490
and just ask Laura to talk a little bit

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.980
about the making of her book,

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.480
Charros: How Mexican
Cowboys are Remapping Race

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:11.840
and American Identity.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.240
Laura?

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.220
- Thank you so much, Steve,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.130
and thanks to my colleagues,
Dan and Ana, for being here.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.070
This idea has been a
long time in the making,

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:21.670
and given COVID, given
the state of the world,

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.130
it took us a while to be together,

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.050
but I'm so happy to be here
and to be in celebration,

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.610
and thank you all for coming.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:32.180
I'm gonna try to share my slides here.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:33.130
One moment, please.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.950
Okay, I'm speaking today,
as Steve mentioned,

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.700
from my new book, Charros:
How Mexican Cowboys

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.541
are Remapping Race and American Identity.

00:04:42.541 --> 00:04:44.050
And in a general sense,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.410
the book is really
tracking the various ways

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.370
in which Mexican immigrant and
Mexican-American communities

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.770
in the United States have mobilized

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.110
to this extremely significant figure

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:58.460
of the charro pursuit of
various political, social,

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.600
and cultural initiatives
in the United States.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.878
And so I just wanna briefly explain

00:05:04.878 --> 00:05:07.987
what a charro is or who a charro is.

00:05:07.987 --> 00:05:11.090
He is probably most familiar

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.146
to two folks because the charro suit,

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:15.760
or the (speaking in foreign language),

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.529
is worn by mariachi musicians.

00:05:18.529 --> 00:05:20.930
But the charro that I am writing about

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.767
in the book is related more to
the acts of horseback riding,

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:29.100
and so, and branching,
and roping, and rodeo.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.350
So the best translation
of the word, charro,

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.040
might be Mexican horseman and, you know,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.980
just an aside, that as the
publisher and I were deciding

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.040
on the title for the book,

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.420
in the subtitle is, Mexican Cowboys.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.280
And I think that's a partial translation

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.520
but it's not a great translation because

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.320
at least in the US the
image we have of a cowboy

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.320
is of a kind of a working
class, isolated, rough figure,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.450
and in Mexico the figure of the charro

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:00.250
is much more associated with the elite,

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.570
with landowners, and with most of all,

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.330
probably most significantly,
with highly skilled

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:08.890
dignified work on horses
with other ranch owners.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:11.590
So it's much more of an
elite or a gentleman,

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.240
the gentlemen horseman might
be a better translation.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.458
And charros have a long history
in Mexico, 400-plus years.

00:06:17.458 --> 00:06:21.390
It's a figure that's associated
with Spanish colonization

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:25.680
and he really has contested
divergent meanings.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.590
So on the one hand, he has been associated

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:30.620
with the elite in Mexico,
the Spanish colonial elite,

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.070
but he also has been associated

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.740
with nationalist revolutionary figures,

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.100
like Emiliano Zapata and Pancho Villa.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.100
And so part of what I was interested in

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.750
are those struggles over
what the charro should mean

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:42.400
in terms of its class status

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.360
and his relationship
to Mexican nationalism.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.940
But the particular contribution of my book

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.130
was to try to track the
ways that those debates

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:54.091
had been carried out in the United States.

00:06:54.091 --> 00:06:56.898
So the charro is very, very
well-studied within Mexico.

00:06:56.898 --> 00:07:00.360
There's great critical
scholarship by Mexican scholars,

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.100
a lot of which I cite in the book,

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:03.410
but he had not been the study

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.810
of significant scholarship in the US,

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.800
and this is despite the fact that charros

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.741
have emerged as political
actors in the United States

00:07:10.741 --> 00:07:13.010
as early as the 1930s

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.940
we did have a couple of good ethnographies

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.850
by anthropologists that had
been conducted in the 1990s

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.220
but there was really no history

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.900
of the ways that charros had been used

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.780
by Mexican-American communities.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.920
And so that was the purpose of my book,

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.940
how Mexican communities
in the US have mobilized

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.280
this complicated and yet
very significant figure

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.133
for a variety of social and
political change initiatives.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.750
Importantly, my book
was not an ethnography.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.410
It is an analysis that is
very much public facing.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.110
I was focused on how
ethnic Mexican communities

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.480
have tried to impact
various public institutions

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.380
and social structures and to
link that to the long history

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:53.920
of dispossession and racialization
in the United States.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.910
And sometimes that involved looking

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:58.460
at how charro communities in the US

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:01.300
had challenged whitewashed mythologies

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:05.110
of ranching and rodeo and
cowboys in the Americas.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.358
And so that is where the
idea of the subtitle,

00:08:08.358 --> 00:08:11.842
how these cowboys,
again, an inadequate term

00:08:11.842 --> 00:08:14.141
have remapped American identity in part

00:08:14.141 --> 00:08:17.970
by invoking this longer
and hemispheric history

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:21.350
of the charro in the US.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:22.810
So I used a variety of sources,

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.920
Spanish and English language newspapers,

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.230
lots of archival collections deposited

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:30.610
in various US institutions,
mostly in the Southwest

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:32.680
by middle-class Mexican-American figures

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:35.310
who were involved with
general associations

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:37.840
or associations of men
who ride and rope together

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.160
and compete together.

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:42.120
A few interviews, looking
at legislative documents,

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.140
school curricula, all kinds of things.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:45.660
It's a very interdisciplinary study.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.770
And a lot of this was about place,

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.500
which we'll talk a
little bit about later on

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.920
in our conversation.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:52.340
And so I just want to show you some

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:56.210
of the specific case studies
that the book is looking at.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.550
I started in Los Angeles where the focus

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:02.120
was really on the ways that the
charro will became a vehicle

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.730
for Mexican-Americans
to integrate the state,

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:07.950
and specifically the Los
Angeles Sheriff's Department.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.590
This is Sheriff Eugene Biscailuz,

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:11.240
who could trace his identity back

00:09:12.083 --> 00:09:13.120
to elite California families

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.850
and who dressed up as a charro

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:18.020
as the head of the Los
Angeles Sheriff's Department.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:20.870
There was also a group of
working class Mexican-Americans

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:24.960
from East LA who joined the
Sheriff's posse in this period.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.943
And this is in the 1940s and 1950s,

00:09:27.811 --> 00:09:29.800
and I think this is a
really important counterpart

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.562
to other studies of Mexican-Americans

00:09:32.562 --> 00:09:35.620
in LA in the war time era

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.500
who were mostly persecuted by the police.

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:41.590
But in this case, charros were
also members of the police.

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:43.400
And you can see that here.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.040
These are LA Sheriff's
Department posse charros

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.460
in the background of this photo.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:52.447
I also have a study of
San Antonio in the 1940s

00:09:52.447 --> 00:09:55.240
and '50s where the charros
were much more focused

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:56.090
on the economy.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.900
So these were folks who were
part of the city's tiny,

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:01.790
tiny middle-class,
Mexican-American middle-class

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.060
but who were uniquely positioned to draw

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.800
on the image and the history

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.500
of the charro to build a
binational tourist economy,

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.340
drawing in investors from Northern Mexico

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.440
at a time when San
Antonio was really trying

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.770
to propel its own urban growth.

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:19.093
And so that gave them a
degree of economic leverage.

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:23.340
I also have some materials on Colorado,

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.810
and in my chapters on
Denver and Pueblo, Colorado,

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:30.650
I really am drawn to this
person, Lena Archuleta,

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:32.350
who was the first Hispana principal

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:35.340
of Denver Public Schools and
who was centrally involved

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:38.290
in the work towards desegregation,
bilingual education,

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:40.880
and culturally responsive
curriculum in part

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:44.249
by offering a curriculum
guide called The Rodeo,

00:10:44.249 --> 00:10:46.130
I'm sorry, it's blocked by my screen,

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:48.290
but It's Rich Spanish-Mexican Heritage.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:51.370
And so for her, in Denver,
the work of the charro,

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:53.374
she was a charro, or a charra,

00:10:53.374 --> 00:10:56.410
this really influential local principal,

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:58.240
and again, drew on the charro as a source

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.490
of empowerment for
Spanish-speaking children.

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:03.450
I also look at the ways that Charreria,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:07.550
or Mexican rodeo could be
used to help ethnic Mexicans

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:10.890
claim public space as they were
suburbanizing in Los Angeles

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.100
and to a lesser extent suburbs
of other Southwestern cities.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:18.410
And here, my hope was to
show how suburbanization

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:21.260
and residential integration is
not only about private space

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.300
but about struggles for public space

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.940
and the expression of a cultural
identity as a way, again,

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.730
of transforming racialized structures.

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:31.280
And I finish the book
By looking at the ways

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:32.690
that charros have been caught up

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:35.070
in animal welfare legislation.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.120
Several of the events in the Mexican rodeo

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.210
have been deeply contested
and controversial,

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:41.860
and they've been subject
to a lot of critique

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:43.810
by animal rights activists,

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:46.200
which has forced them into
the legislative sphere.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.060
And so my last chapter looks

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.390
at the ways that those
debates about animal welfare

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.260
have have really led
charros to scale up away

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:56.590
from the local level and become
national political actors,

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:58.260
regional and national political actors

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:00.860
in a way that they become
formal political subjects.

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:03.640
I'm going to just close
by saying something

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:05.540
about how I came to this project,

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:07.570
and I'll stop my screen
share for a second.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:11.766
So I came to this
project because I grew up

00:12:11.766 --> 00:12:16.560
in the suburbs of Los Angeles
in the 1980s and 1990s.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.880
And this was a time, as
Dan has written about

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.500
in his first book, Racial Propositions,

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:24.640
that was deeply, deeply
contentious around issues

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.380
of race and immigration.

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:28.510
The way that I experienced
those was that I grew up

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:31.010
in a protected rural neighborhood

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:33.200
in the city of Los Angeles
where horse-keeping

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.750
was protected, where the
rights to keep chickens

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:38.440
and other farm animals was
protected by city government,

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.820
and it was also as a
neighborhood that was something

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.420
like 90% white at a time when the city

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:46.450
of LA was becoming a majority Latinx,

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:49.930
like less than 30% white
at the county level.

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:52.410
And so I became very
interested in the relationships

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:55.490
between the city's protection
of rural landscapes

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.930
and the unbelievably
disproportionate whiteness

00:12:57.930 --> 00:12:58.980
of that neighborhood.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.930
And so I wrote about
that in my first book,

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:02.500
Making the San Fernando Valley.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.590
But it was also clear
even in those moments that

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:06.950
that was never the whole story.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:08.880
And in fact, for this book,

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.040
I was really drawing on my own experiences

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.020
as a child growing up riding
around my neighborhood

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:17.830
and meeting out in public
space charros who were

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:21.850
riding their horses pride
fully with dignity, with skill.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:25.260
And I also, so I was able
to see that in public space

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.300
in my neighborhood, my
majority white neighborhood,

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.950
but these were folks from
adjacent neighborhoods.

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:32.940
But also I was able to see how
threatened local white folks

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.470
in my neighborhood were by
those alternative histories

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:37.930
of of horsemanship and skill.

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:41.200
And so this book was really
about trying to untangle

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.750
those relationships and those tensions.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:46.390
And I'll just say as a way of wrapping up

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:48.910
it has been amazing to me to realize

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:50.670
that I have spent the last 15 years

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:52.467
of my life and at least two books

00:13:52.467 --> 00:13:55.440
and some articles basically
trying to figure out

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.640
the tensions and the
contradictions of my childhood.

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:59.160
And I think that's one of the things

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.230
that ethnic studies
scholarship and scholarship

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:03.700
in general can allow us to do.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:04.770
So thank you very much.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.521
I'll pass it over to Dan.

00:14:06.521 --> 00:14:08.707
- Thanks so much, Laura.

00:14:08.707 --> 00:14:11.580
I so appreciate like in your work,

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.360
just how rigorous the balance is

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:15.560
between like history and place-based,

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:17.110
then it moves across all these sites,

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:18.950
and how you've just
narrated its connection

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:20.310
to your own experiences.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.290
So just again, thanks to Steve and RITM

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:26.290
and folks who are listening today.

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:29.567
And I'll just say, you know,
in terms of like methodology

00:14:29.567 --> 00:14:32.990
for especially students
listening, if you're looking

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:36.030
for good models and examples,
please follow Laura and Ana.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:39.046
Because the only methodology
that I can share here

00:14:39.046 --> 00:14:42.243
is kind of looking at things politically

00:14:42.243 --> 00:14:45.140
that have taken place in the
last 10 years that relate

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.414
to the kind of bottom dropping out,

00:14:47.414 --> 00:14:50.420
it seems, of our social life and economy

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:52.730
and fabric and the ways
they've intersected

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:57.730
with these kind of unusual
developments connected

00:14:57.913 --> 00:14:59.430
to racial politics.

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:01.560
So I just thought to try to share some

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:03.990
of like how that works for us.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:05.607
I would just share some of these images

00:15:05.607 --> 00:15:07.980
and kind of talk about what for us

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.690
are always just questions
on top of questions,

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:12.547
on top of questions.

00:15:12.547 --> 00:15:17.029
So this is the book, Producers,
Parasites, and Patriots,

00:15:17.029 --> 00:15:19.060
and before I was at Yale,

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:20.630
I was at the University
of Oregon for 10 years.

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:24.060
So that's really where
this took root in 2012

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:25.760
right after the midterm elections.

00:15:26.729 --> 00:15:29.100
My co-author Joe Lowndes
and I tried to figure out

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:30.787
some of these developments.

00:15:30.787 --> 00:15:32.470
And I'll just start with
this since this group,

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:34.500
the Proud Boys, was mentioned for those

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.430
that might've watched the
presidential debates last night.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:41.330
So a group that was started
in 2016 by Gavin McInnes,

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:44.940
co-founder of VICE Magazine and Media

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.040
which kind of represented itself as a,

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:50.030
what they call as a Western
chauvinist organization.

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:51.400
So they certainly would disavow

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:53.460
that they were white supremacists as such,

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:58.460
and yet constantly
involved in not just kind

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:00.880
of like street level
violence and fighting,

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:03.930
but actually they became the
kind of like street force

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:06.000
of Trumpism in the
aftermath of the election.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.480
And you'll see here, these are photos

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:09.590
from different Proud Boys gatherings.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:11.360
And you know what startling here

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:14.350
is the three-fingered Okay symbol,

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:16.320
which is now generally
taken up to be a symbol

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.470
of white supremacy offered
up by not just, you know,

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:24.050
a man of who color seemed to
like wandered into the photo,

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:27.060
but men of color who seem much
more central to this project.

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.610
And so part of what we know is,

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:31.810
this is not all of the book but, you know,

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:34.360
part of the book was
thinking about what we call

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:37.630
the kind of multiracial
or multicultural far-right

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:42.060
that has emerged since
2016, that goes hand in hand

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:44.700
with the most white supremacist tendencies

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.160
of the Trump administration
and of conservatism today.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:50.740
And yet, you know, from our perspective,

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.280
didn't have good accounts
to think about kind

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.230
of how this was happening
or where this came from.

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:56.840
So in the lower left corner,

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:59.700
this is a photo of the
Proud Boys in New York City.

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:01.513
You'll see a photo on the right,

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:05.150
or image of not only just
all these men of color,

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:09.500
but actually like self-described
Guatemalan skinheads,

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:12.220
immigrant skinheads engaged
in immigrant bashing

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:13.370
in New York City.

00:17:13.370 --> 00:17:15.640
So again, a lot for us
to get our heads around,

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:17.700
and I'll talk a little bit
more about where we ended up.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.810
But these are some of
what we kind of took up.

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:22.230
I would say, you know, during the book,

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:23.780
we didn't conduct too many interviews,

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:28.100
but you know, really the
most kind of vexing moment

00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:30.260
was when we talked to
this figure in the middle

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:33.103
of Joey Gibson, who at
the time was running for,

00:17:34.064 --> 00:17:36.040
he was a kind of outsider candidate

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:39.332
for a Republican Senate seat in Washington

00:17:39.332 --> 00:17:43.070
and had just founded a
group called Patriot Prayer

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:44.520
which has been very, very active

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.350
in these kinds of street
brawls in Portland.

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:49.653
And we asked him like, well,

00:17:49.653 --> 00:17:50.810
where do your politics come from?

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.640
Like who's influenced you?

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:54.740
And Gibson went on this long

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:58.515
and very serious and
sincere account of Dr. King,

00:17:58.515 --> 00:18:01.420
of the Greensboro lunch counter sit-ins

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:04.150
and their capacity to
withstand state violence

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.030
and their willingness to challenge it.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:08.960
He talked about the tragedy
of the war on drugs,

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:11.200
and again, how the state has reached in

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:13.760
and disrupted the lives
of so many black families

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:14.860
and communities.

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.880
You had a critique of big pharma.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.508
And so this was a strange, you know,

00:18:19.508 --> 00:18:22.478
we left the interview like,
is this just kind of mouthing,

00:18:22.478 --> 00:18:25.420
you know, if you were a progressive

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.750
and self-identified kind of anti-racist,

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:30.230
these would be predictable
kind of significations.

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:34.640
But for Gibson, he was
really in this active process

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:37.190
of thinking about how certain traditions

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:39.620
of the Civil Rights movement
might be incorporated

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:43.074
into what was a very
grassroots far-right project.

00:18:43.074 --> 00:18:46.200
So these are some of the
topics we take up in the book,

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:47.590
especially in the latter chapters,

00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:49.550
about the kind of
multiculturalism of the far-right.

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:52.970
We write about this figure
on the right, Sonnie Johnson,

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.650
who was a close confidant
of Andrew Breitbart

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:58.670
at Breitbart Media, very
close to Steve Bannon,

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:02.490
a movement builder within
very conservative circles.

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:05.570
Has her own weekly radio
show on Breitbart Radio

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:08.480
that one can listen to if you
have Sirius Satellite Radio

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.350
that I highly, highly recommend.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.370
She's really an anti-racist
figure on the right

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:15.480
who has a vision of that the far-right

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:19.400
might be cleansed of the
kind of racial antagonisms

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.900
that's been associated
with in the last 40 years,

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:24.890
in particular, and that
essentially black communities

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:28.100
could be the heart of a new pro-business,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:30.620
militarized kind of anti-choice,

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:33.384
anti-feminist right-wing project

00:19:33.384 --> 00:19:37.290
in which black people
would be the exemplars

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:38.540
of those commitments.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:41.410
But there are just some
other examples images.

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:43.350
So, you know, these
groups would often like go

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:45.370
to kind of typical liberal setting,

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:47.720
in this case, Evergreen State University.

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:49.050
And you can see, you know,

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.580
these openly Islamophobic and violent,

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:54.000
it says United States ISIS Hunting Permit,

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.316
and then this kind of provocation.

00:19:56.316 --> 00:19:58.320
Yeah, we're a racist group
that accepts accepts members

00:19:58.320 --> 00:19:59.153
of all races.

00:19:59.153 --> 00:20:01.110
But in fact, that's
precisely what they are.

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:03.650
They are a racist group on the far-right

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:04.797
that is multi-racial in some ways.

00:20:04.797 --> 00:20:06.920
You know, it reminds us that it's not,

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:09.360
the critique is not just,
well, if the Klan had

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:11.497
a diversity initiative
and were more inclusive,

00:20:11.497 --> 00:20:13.490
then they would be, you know,

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:16.180
kind of like politically there.

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:18.977
It's also the substance
of what they talk about

00:20:18.977 --> 00:20:21.500
and that they're dynamic in this way.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:24.470
This is Enrique Tarrio,
Afro-Cuban based in Miami,

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:28.230
who's the head, the nominal
head of the Proud Boys now.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:30.410
This is a picture with
him and Roger Stone again

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:31.500
showing a white power sign.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:33.680
So how are these sutures
and these connections

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:36.010
being made in kind of realtime?

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:37.060
Just a couple more images.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:38.130
This is Diamond and Silk,

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:41.270
who often have appeared on
the campaign trail with Trump,

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:43.630
helping shore up his kind
of nativist commitment,

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:46.810
suggesting, and this certainly
has no correspondence

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:50.137
to actual political behavior,
but this argument that,

00:20:50.137 --> 00:20:52.040
you know, they have this line,

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:54.089
Clinton could do mass incarceration,

00:20:54.089 --> 00:20:56.450
we can do mass deportation.

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:58.930
So a very kind of tricky, again,

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:00.510
kind of suturings that are happening.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:03.550
And, you know, another like,
Whoa, what is going on?

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:08.240
This is Paul Nehlen who ran
for a GOP seat in Wisconsin,

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:11.070
and he was a pretty
unapologetic white supremacist.

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:13.660
He was like, you know, they
discovered these connections

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:16.200
he had and he kind of admitted them.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:19.190
He had Diamond and Silk
do a commercial for him,

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:20.840
which, you know, he used to try to talk

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:22.370
about protecting American jobs.

00:21:22.370 --> 00:21:25.410
His rabid all white base
loved the appearance

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:27.190
of Diamond and Silk, partly, you know,

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:31.370
because it deflected perhaps
charges their own racial animus

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:33.520
but much more because
from their perspective,

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:37.539
they really animated this
vision of a US nationalist,

00:21:37.539 --> 00:21:41.420
militarized nativist formation
that could be multiracial.

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:42.530
And I don't want to say,

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:45.460
this is not targeted in any
way towards black voters.

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:48.830
This is for a white
voters on the far-right?

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:51.030
This is Steve Bannon who we found actually

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.840
was continually making
these appeals and addresses

00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:56.427
to black businessmen and
conservatives in particular

00:21:56.427 --> 00:22:00.236
about how once the kind of era
of globalism and US support

00:22:00.236 --> 00:22:03.574
for neo-liberalism ended,
hundreds of billions

00:22:03.574 --> 00:22:07.640
of dollars could be invested
into black communities

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:09.903
and the traction he got in that claim.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:12.540
And that while we're
more familiar, you know,

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:14.760
people have seen Kanye or Candace Owens,

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.780
who's actually a much
different kind of figure,

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:20.887
there is a small, but
potent bench of black

00:22:20.887 --> 00:22:24.869
and Latinx conservatives
in particular who really

00:22:24.869 --> 00:22:29.869
have a vision for how a new
Republican party might be built

00:22:30.015 --> 00:22:32.540
in the wake of this and who are as far

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:33.700
to the right as Trump.

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:35.110
This is Senator Tim Scott,

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:37.720
beloved by white Tea Party
members in South Carolina,

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:40.730
far more than someone like Lindsey Graham.

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:44.300
Mia Love. a Republican
Congresswoman for four years.

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:46.370
This is her speaking at a gun rights rally

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:48.320
in Salt Lake City.

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:51.589
You can see Confederate flags
and militia flags there.

00:22:51.589 --> 00:22:54.920
You know, I just want to share this image.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:57.600
It's a little distorted, but
this is from a web graphic.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:59.570
It says, "Which is the
party of responsibility?"

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:01.425
Republican, it shows Love.

00:23:01.425 --> 00:23:05.510
By age 30, married, two kids,
Saratoga Springs Council.

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:08.190
This is Sandra Fluke, who
the Democrats had asked

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:12.770
to testify around access to birth control.

00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:17.690
And so in this vision,
Love is the producer,

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:20.860
the good proper patriotic subject,

00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:23.290
and Fluke is the irresponsible kind

00:23:23.290 --> 00:23:26.220
of feminist subject who needs discipline.

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:29.270
So these are just configurations
we would not have expected.

00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:30.130
I'll just end here.

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:31.700
This is Kay Coles James.

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:33.310
She's now president, a black woman,

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:34.500
of the Heritage Foundation,

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:38.560
really the conservative kind
of like compass on the right.

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:41.220
It continues to be as
conservative as you would imagine,

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:42.930
all-white, as you can see in this photo,

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:45.430
but led by a long time kind

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:47.778
of conservative movement builder.

00:23:47.778 --> 00:23:49.074
So I'll just wrap up to say,

00:23:49.074 --> 00:23:50.120
because I felt like I
wanted to say something

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.440
by way of like implications here,

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:54.250
that the incorporation of people of color

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:56.510
on the right actually and paradoxically

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:58.670
makes white supremacy more durable.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:01.010
It shores up and legitimates
its core features

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:04.120
of militarism, patriarchy,
nationalism, and hierarchy,

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:05.740
the inevitability of hierarchy.

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:08.010
These incorporations are
really quite central,

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:10.520
surprisingly, to Trumpism,
and they can exist

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:13.230
with Trump's more open
white nationalist appeals

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:15.710
and they are not
collapsing under the weight

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:17.240
of their own contradictions.

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:19.540
And as we think, regardless of
what happens in the election,

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:21.460
all the kinds of
reconstructions and things

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:24.210
that will happen, this is
something that's not going away.

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:26.270
And I'll just end because we do talk

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:28.120
about a lot of the grassroots organizing

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:30.620
that's happening on the
other side to re-imagine

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:35.000
and rebuild social relations
that could contest this,

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:36.850
particularly, in rural
areas, this wonderful group

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:38.835
the Rural Organizing Project.

00:24:38.835 --> 00:24:39.810
Okay, so that's just a snapshot,

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:42.051
and as chaotic and frenzied as I sound,

00:24:42.051 --> 00:24:46.670
that's partly our methodology in the book.

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:48.540
And I'm going to turn it over to you

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:51.473
so they can hear about a
proper academic project.

00:24:52.666 --> 00:24:54.720
- I don't know if a
proper academic project,

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:59.720
but he's been wonderful to
hear both Laura and Dan's work.

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:03.300
Oftentimes even though we
share meetings and all of that,

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:05.600
we have very little time to really share

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:08.410
our respective academic projects.

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:10.830
So this is really a treat.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:15.820
So my book is really almost
like an outlier in terms

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:18.983
of my broader work that
I've done throughout

00:25:18.983 --> 00:25:19.816
(speaking faintly)

00:25:19.816 --> 00:25:23.011
Most of the time, in
most of the other work,

00:25:23.011 --> 00:25:25.497
I focused on working
class Latinx communities

00:25:25.497 --> 00:25:29.196
in Chicago and in Newark.

00:25:29.196 --> 00:25:31.640
And that has been really the center

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:33.780
of the work that I started doing as part

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:37.100
of my graduate dissertation,
my doctoral dissertation,

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:41.080
and all the way until about 2012

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:44.610
when I decided to explore
the other side of inequality

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:46.800
and started to try to understand not only

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:51.640
how inequality damages
the possibilities of some,

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:54.720
but also how it actually benefits others.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:57.255
And to do that, I decided
to do an ethnography

00:25:57.255 --> 00:26:01.013
that involved participant
observation, interviews,

00:26:01.013 --> 00:26:03.335
and lots of different methodologies

00:26:03.335 --> 00:26:05.410
including some archival work.

00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:08.550
And it is a comparative ethnography

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:10.990
of two neighborhoods,

00:26:10.990 --> 00:26:14.690
two elite wealthy mostly
white neighborhoods.

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:17.630
One of them is Ipanema, in Rio de Janeiro,

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:20.769
and the other one is El
Condado, in Puerto Rico.

00:26:20.769 --> 00:26:25.143
I spent about a month in each place

00:26:25.143 --> 00:26:29.730
and my friends were really
joking with me about it.

00:26:29.730 --> 00:26:31.870
They were like, Oh, wow, you
went from walking the streets

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:35.510
of Newark to like really
hanging out at the beaches

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:39.620
of the Caribbean and the
Atlantic coast of South America.

00:26:39.620 --> 00:26:43.247
And the truth was that a
lot of times anthropologists

00:26:43.247 --> 00:26:48.020
and ethnographers are associated
with deep hanging out,

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:49.740
what we call it deep hanging out.

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:52.880
So part of the research
that you do really involves

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:54.690
trying to not only interview people,

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:57.118
but see if what they
do in practice actually

00:26:57.118 --> 00:27:00.853
supports what they tell you that they do.

00:27:00.853 --> 00:27:03.672
So that's part of why we spend
so much time with people.

00:27:03.672 --> 00:27:06.930
One of the things that I wanted to,

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:08.740
the reason why I chose,

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:10.120
there were several reasons why I chose

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:12.200
these two specific sites,

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:14.050
but what I found interesting
since we're talking

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:18.982
about the actual, you know,
the background of bookmaking,

00:27:18.982 --> 00:27:21.659
is that I got quite a bit of pushback

00:27:21.659 --> 00:27:26.030
for assuming Puerto Rico to
be part of the Latin-American

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:30.358
and Caribbean geography rather
than being part of the US.

00:27:30.358 --> 00:27:33.850
There was a very strong push
for me to turn this book

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:36.757
into a US/Brazil comparison,

00:27:36.757 --> 00:27:38.520
even if I did go to Puerto Rico,

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:40.940
and I had to really
push back against that,

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:43.330
and I felt very strongly
that I just wanted

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:46.791
to remove Puerto Rico for from
the side of exceptionalism

00:27:46.791 --> 00:27:50.734
where it's neither cover by
American Studies oftentimes

00:27:50.734 --> 00:27:53.170
or by Latin American studies because both

00:27:53.170 --> 00:27:56.340
of them feel that it doesn't
fit quite into either one.

00:27:56.340 --> 00:28:00.462
So mostly I did my work
among very wealthy parents.

00:28:00.462 --> 00:28:04.300
And the reason why I chose
parenting was because

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:08.040
I really wanted to study not
people's necessarily income

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:13.030
or economic wealth in a
traditional Marxist sense,

00:28:13.030 --> 00:28:17.860
but also in terms of how
they made the decisions,

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:21.043
our money and our own resources moral?

00:28:21.043 --> 00:28:24.821
How do they explain that they
have so much more than others?

00:28:24.821 --> 00:28:28.780
It was a really challenging
project because I really,

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:31.410
all the time I was trying not to judge,

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:32.810
which is part of what we try to, you know,

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:35.820
in anthropology, we try
to sort of step back

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:38.380
and let people be who they
are and sort of analyze

00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:42.320
what we get there, although
that's not always possible.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:47.320
So one of the things that
I did, that I found out

00:28:47.651 --> 00:28:51.130
was also a contradiction.

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:52.480
Like when Dan was talking,

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:54.930
it made me realize that it was
really a big contradiction.

00:28:54.930 --> 00:28:57.600
I mean, here we have very wealthy parents

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:00.040
who are very anti-consumption.

00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:02.260
They really don't want their
kids to have everything.

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:03.760
They're very selective in terms

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:05.790
of what they give to children.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:07.530
So they're actually not the people

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:09.551
that you would think they are.

00:29:09.551 --> 00:29:11.510
They're not, I think I
went in there expecting,

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:14.280
you know, all these very composed,

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.230
and people just wearing
all these brand names,

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:18.800
and all these other things.

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:20.320
And in reality, they were not at all.

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:23.190
They were the kind of people
that were very invested

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:27.140
in what I call inner
inner world cultivation,

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:29.740
which means that they
develop their whiteness

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:31.883
as an interiority project.

00:29:31.883 --> 00:29:35.596
And so, whiteness for
them is not necessarily

00:29:35.596 --> 00:29:40.596
about displaying certain fashion
or certain body practices,

00:29:41.491 --> 00:29:45.980
but also about being able to
express who they are as people.

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:50.980
And so, there were several
things that sort of,

00:29:52.815 --> 00:29:57.273
that were difficult to put up,

00:29:57.273 --> 00:29:59.050
(speaking faintly)

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:01.000
in terms of the actual project

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:04.570
of writing the book and turning
this project into a book.

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:08.960
I think one of the main ones
that happened very later

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:12.063
on in the process was even
the choice of the cover.

00:30:12.063 --> 00:30:16.634
So the cover was a back and forth fight

00:30:16.634 --> 00:30:20.393
with whoever decides
on covers at the press,

00:30:20.393 --> 00:30:25.333
and by the way, I worked
with these Latinx editors,

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:27.493
two of them.

00:30:27.493 --> 00:30:29.280
I was like, so blessed with that.

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:31.950
I was just like, really,
they were exceptional.

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:34.650
But you know, when you're
dealing with the presses,

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:36.260
there are a lot of different departments.

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:38.850
And I remember they insisted

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:43.520
that I put a nanny and
a child in the cover.

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:46.870
And for me, I really wanted these to be

00:30:46.870 --> 00:30:49.550
about the wealthy parents,
because a lot of times,

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:52.668
what happens in anthropological
work is that we protect,

00:30:52.668 --> 00:30:56.060
you know, we protect the wealthy

00:30:56.060 --> 00:30:59.430
and very much analyze the
poor and working classes.

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:02.468
So what I wanted was to make sure

00:31:02.468 --> 00:31:04.730
that they understood this is not a project

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:05.840
about the nannies.

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:08.720
This is actually a project
about the wealthy people,

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:10.670
and I just wanted the
wealthy people on the cover.

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:12.970
And of course I didn't, I
turned out to be that diva

00:31:12.970 --> 00:31:15.030
because everybody, you
know, thinks then like,

00:31:15.030 --> 00:31:17.140
Oh my God, she's just like, hey, whatever.

00:31:17.140 --> 00:31:20.310
But anyway, so in the
end that worked out and,

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:22.624
you know, that was one of the things that

00:31:22.624 --> 00:31:24.324
I kind of pretty much insisted on.

00:31:25.453 --> 00:31:28.050
I think one of the main issues

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:30.470
with a project like this,
which is ethnographic,

00:31:30.470 --> 00:31:32.450
is that you bring your biography with you.

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:35.000
And of course that's true in every project

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:36.910
to some degree or another.

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:39.080
And in this particular case,

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:44.080
it was really a tough
project to be part of,

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:47.870
not for the reason, you
know, it was tough because,

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:50.570
obviously the entrance
piece of like trying

00:31:50.570 --> 00:31:52.860
to get in touch with people
that are this wealthy,

00:31:52.860 --> 00:31:54.610
the wealthy are very protected.

00:31:54.610 --> 00:31:57.460
So how do you get in touch with them?

00:31:57.460 --> 00:32:02.350
Once that is obtained, once that happens,

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:04.550
one of the things that
becomes really difficult

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:09.090
is to try to learn to be at
ease with this particular crowd

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:12.808
that you are working with and, you know,

00:32:12.808 --> 00:32:14.964
that are your interlocutors.

00:32:14.964 --> 00:32:19.964
And so that those are parts of the project

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:22.192
that I worked throughout.

00:32:22.192 --> 00:32:25.050
Of course, you know, I was very aware

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:29.360
of where the nodules of privilege were

00:32:29.360 --> 00:32:33.220
in the El Condado side since
I grew up in Puerto Rico,

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:36.820
and I grew up in not in
any of those circles,

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:37.960
but I knew all of them.

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:39.790
Like, you know, everybody kind of knew

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:44.210
where the good schools were and
where the country clubs were

00:32:44.210 --> 00:32:45.300
and all of that.

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:48.220
And then in Brazil, the
challenges actually were different

00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:49.970
because I was very accepted as somebody

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:52.280
who was not from there,
so everybody wanted

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:53.620
to show me their lives.

00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:56.890
But at the same time,
it was also this kind

00:32:56.890 --> 00:33:01.698
of consistently trying
to understand people

00:33:01.698 --> 00:33:04.200
for who they were in their context

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:06.180
that they were, you know,
where they were showing me,

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:09.200
and at the same time, develop
an analytical take on them,

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:10.640
on the project.

00:33:10.640 --> 00:33:14.360
So I see both of the, a lot
of people have asked me,

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:17.700
you know, why Brazil
is such a big country,

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:19.070
Puerto Rico is such a small country,

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:20.993
like all these other things.

00:33:20.993 --> 00:33:24.380
And I explained, in the
book I spent quite some time

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:27.280
explaining why I feel that these units,

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:30.397
the neighborhood units are comparable.

00:33:30.397 --> 00:33:34.310
I don't know how to share my PowerPoint

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:36.890
but let me see if I can just, Oh, okay,

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:41.170
actually that's pretty, yeah,
so I'm not going to share

00:33:42.036 --> 00:33:43.927
very many of the slides.

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:50.530
I just want to show
the cover that I chose.

00:33:53.540 --> 00:33:56.170
But I also want to show the similarities

00:33:56.170 --> 00:33:59.670
of the two neighborhoods
that I focused on.

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:03.470
So on the left, there
is, I wish I could point,

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:05.410
but so on the left is El Condado.

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:08.380
As you can see, there
is a lagoon where a lot

00:34:08.380 --> 00:34:11.600
of the wealthy people
spend their time kayaking,

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:14.470
doing water sports, all of that.

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:18.132
At the other side is the
Atlantic ocean and the beach,

00:34:18.132 --> 00:34:21.100
and that also created some luxury culture

00:34:21.100 --> 00:34:24.320
that was very much
present among these elites

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:26.154
and in their parenting.

00:34:26.154 --> 00:34:30.728
Ipanema on the right, if
you look at both places

00:34:30.728 --> 00:34:33.710
from the top, there's
quite a bit of similarities

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:35.890
in the landscape of each.

00:34:35.890 --> 00:34:40.800
And one of the things that
I center on is how does

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:44.666
the geographies, particular
geography that has become

00:34:44.666 --> 00:34:48.440
associated with wellness
and healthy living

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:50.610
and fitness and all these other things

00:34:53.058 --> 00:34:56.065
enters the world of parenting?

00:34:56.065 --> 00:35:00.790
One thing also that I wanted
to examine through the lens

00:35:00.790 --> 00:35:03.450
of parenting was the bigger
question of sovereignty,

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:08.450
and also of the language of corruption.

00:35:09.407 --> 00:35:14.077
And so, that became very evident once

00:35:14.077 --> 00:35:15.710
I was talking to people.

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:17.610
At this point, both in
Brazil and Puerto Rico,

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:20.770
there were some, you
know, the countries were

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:23.017
in a severe economic crisis,

00:35:23.017 --> 00:35:25.880
most people talked about the government

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:27.530
in terms of corruption,

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:30.910
and all of the language became intertwined

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:34.570
with how people view their
way of raising children.

00:35:34.570 --> 00:35:35.570
And of course, you know,

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:39.160
when you put children in the,
anything becomes justified,

00:35:39.160 --> 00:35:41.039
and that's part of why child,

00:35:41.039 --> 00:35:43.550
you know, talking about your children

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:45.610
is particularly powerful
because, you know,

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:47.920
nobody will criticize these wealthy people

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:53.691
for wanting to do, to
protect their children.

00:35:53.691 --> 00:35:58.691
So I think that's all that I want to,

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:02.390
that I want to share.

00:36:02.390 --> 00:36:05.468
Let me see, I don't know how
I get out of the sharing.

00:36:05.468 --> 00:36:07.233
I just do this.

00:36:09.187 --> 00:36:10.787
Sorry about that.

00:36:10.787 --> 00:36:15.053
So, yeah, so I'm looking forward
to hearing your questions.

00:36:16.691 --> 00:36:19.013
- Thank you, Ana, and thank you, Dan,

00:36:19.013 --> 00:36:22.050
and thank you, Laura,
for these presentations.

00:36:22.050 --> 00:36:25.420
I'm going to remind our audience members,

00:36:25.420 --> 00:36:27.607
if you have a question, to
please put them in the Q&amp;A

00:36:27.607 --> 00:36:30.832
and we'll do our best to get
to as many of them as we can.

00:36:30.832 --> 00:36:34.010
You know, Ana, you shared with us a bit

00:36:34.010 --> 00:36:37.460
of the geography of
Ipanema and of El Condado,

00:36:37.460 --> 00:36:39.830
and I wanted to take that
question in a sense to Laura

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:42.310
who of course, is a geographer.

00:36:42.310 --> 00:36:44.820
And her book, as she mentioned briefly,

00:36:44.820 --> 00:36:47.760
covers a lot of territory
and it thinks comparatively

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:50.460
about charros and their
practices in a number

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:52.810
of different places,
California, Texas, Colorado,

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:56.750
as she mentioned, but also
Oklahoma, Kansas, and Nevada,

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:58.090
and perhaps elsewhere.

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:01.060
So Laura, maybe you could
talk a little bit about

00:37:01.060 --> 00:37:04.235
how your book uses a comparative
geographical approach,

00:37:04.235 --> 00:37:07.560
and you know, what the
challenges of that approach

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:10.080
were for you and the
importance of that approach

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:12.490
were in the writing of this book.

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:13.323
- Thank you.

00:37:13.323 --> 00:37:16.310
Yeah, I should say that when
I first started this book

00:37:16.310 --> 00:37:19.730
I had no idea where it was
going to go geographically.

00:37:19.730 --> 00:37:21.580
I had one story I had stumbled

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:23.150
upon in the archives about a group

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:26.288
of suburban charros in
Los Angeles in the 1970s.

00:37:26.288 --> 00:37:28.552
But I didn't know how to,

00:37:28.552 --> 00:37:30.450
I didn't know how that was
going to become a book.

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:32.876
It was clear that that story
alone was not the book.

00:37:32.876 --> 00:37:35.490
As I started to share the project

00:37:35.490 --> 00:37:37.570
and just the general interest

00:37:37.570 --> 00:37:39.330
in charros with friends, with colleagues,

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:40.330
with family members,

00:37:41.327 --> 00:37:43.050
they started to send
me newspapers articles

00:37:43.050 --> 00:37:45.360
or things that they had
found in the archives,

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:47.620
and that led me to places like San Antonio

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:50.678
that I really had very
little knowledge about,

00:37:50.678 --> 00:37:52.580
and then sent me to Denver,

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:54.730
and I started just stumbling
across these things.

00:37:54.730 --> 00:37:57.390
And it became very clear
to me that the charros

00:37:57.390 --> 00:37:59.678
in these various places were not the same.

00:37:59.678 --> 00:38:01.510
They were not the same kind

00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:03.170
of people in terms of class status.

00:38:03.170 --> 00:38:04.720
In some places they were working class,

00:38:04.720 --> 00:38:06.760
in some places they were middle-class.

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:09.340
In some places they
were mostly immigrants,

00:38:09.340 --> 00:38:12.004
and other places they had
their families had been

00:38:12.004 --> 00:38:14.603
in the US, like in New
Mexico since the 1700s.

00:38:15.552 --> 00:38:17.730
And so I at first was
really quite overwhelmed

00:38:17.730 --> 00:38:19.060
by trying to make sense of this,

00:38:19.060 --> 00:38:22.880
how this figure had been used
in so many different ways

00:38:22.880 --> 00:38:24.900
by such different groups of people.

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:26.710
I started to wonder how those differences

00:38:26.710 --> 00:38:28.400
that I was observing might relate

00:38:28.400 --> 00:38:31.500
to the specific geographies
of ethnic Mexican people

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:33.465
in different parts of the US.

00:38:33.465 --> 00:38:36.370
And I wanted, I eventually
came to the conclusion

00:38:36.370 --> 00:38:38.730
that whatever Charreria and the activism

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:40.760
of charros looked like in different places

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:44.650
was in almost all cases related
to the specific processes

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:46.890
through which ethnic
Mexicans had been subjugated

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:47.723
in those places.

00:38:47.723 --> 00:38:49.590
So in San Antonio, it made sense to me,

00:38:49.590 --> 00:38:51.290
or it later made sense to me

00:38:51.290 --> 00:38:52.880
that the focus was on the economy

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:55.590
because of the violence of
the economy in South Texas

00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:58.710
and the fact that ethnic Mexican
people had been so confined

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:00.664
to farm labor, for example.

00:39:00.664 --> 00:39:02.710
And so, whereas in Denver,

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:05.800
it took the form of education
for a variety of reasons.

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:07.450
And so for me, that became the project,

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:10.840
was to try to explain
how and why the activism

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:13.920
around the charro looked
so different as a way

00:39:13.920 --> 00:39:16.300
to map out the different trajectories

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:18.210
of ethnic Mexican people in various places

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:19.470
across the United States.

00:39:19.470 --> 00:39:21.900
And I think this is something
that we see a little bit

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:23.850
in Latinx studies for other figures

00:39:23.850 --> 00:39:26.339
like the BRAH-SEHR-ROH
and AH-CHOOK-OH, you know,

00:39:26.339 --> 00:39:28.570
that I would like to see more of,

00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:30.712
because we know, for
example, LAH-LOH LEE-KOHN

00:39:30.712 --> 00:39:32.750
who's at the University of Wisconsin,

00:39:32.750 --> 00:39:34.020
did a little bit of work, for example,

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:36.080
on Pachecos in Tucson and
how that was different

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:36.913
in Los Angeles.

00:39:36.913 --> 00:39:38.960
I think this is something
that's really important

00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:43.150
to think about when we're
studying any racialized community.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:45.550
The challenge of this was that I did not

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:47.880
have lived experience
in most of these places.

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:50.450
The LA chapters were super easy to, well,

00:39:50.450 --> 00:39:51.930
a book chapters never that easy to write,

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:54.430
but they were much easier to write.

00:39:54.430 --> 00:39:56.530
The other places, sometimes,
I had never been to

00:39:56.530 --> 00:39:58.330
or I'd only been to for a few hours.

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:01.441
And so what I did was I read deeply.

00:40:01.441 --> 00:40:05.030
For graduate students on the webinar

00:40:05.030 --> 00:40:06.770
or people who have been graduate students,

00:40:06.770 --> 00:40:08.700
it felt like I was doing an oral spiel

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:09.880
for each of these places.

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:13.220
You know, I spent a year
reading about Colorado,

00:40:13.220 --> 00:40:15.935
ethnic Mexican people in Colorado.

00:40:15.935 --> 00:40:18.730
I tried to spend time in
those places whenever I could.

00:40:18.730 --> 00:40:21.480
I spent one memorable
day in Pueblo, Colorado,

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:23.150
which was not enough.

00:40:23.150 --> 00:40:24.220
Nut it was really important

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:26.360
to get to these places and to feel them,

00:40:26.360 --> 00:40:28.560
to feel, and this sounds cheesy,

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:30.880
but to feel what the
land feels like there,

00:40:30.880 --> 00:40:33.060
and the spatial arrangements of things,

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:34.430
and to try to understand how

00:40:34.430 --> 00:40:36.813
that might've affected the ways that folks

00:40:36.813 --> 00:40:38.780
were mobilizing in the past.

00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:40.080
And finally, I tried to talk

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:41.650
to people who were from those places.

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:44.690
You know, one day I took the
train down from Yale's campus

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:48.119
to Grand Central Station
and met with Maria Montoya,

00:40:48.119 --> 00:40:51.510
a historian, a Western
historian who's now at NYU,

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:54.170
and I just, I was like,
did I get it right,

00:40:54.170 --> 00:40:55.420
did I get Colorado right,

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:58.310
and I asked, you know, for
her honest feedback on that.

00:40:58.310 --> 00:41:00.690
But I think that was
the hardest part of it,

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:02.570
was trying to learn about
all these different places

00:41:02.570 --> 00:41:04.470
and their different
histories of racialization

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:05.780
and subjugation and dispossession,

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:09.110
but also political activism
and to feel, you know,

00:41:09.110 --> 00:41:11.730
some degree of confidence in articulating

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:13.030
those different histories.

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:16.253
- Thanks, Laura.

00:41:16.253 --> 00:41:18.490
Dan, let me ask you a question that may be

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:20.577
in part about your methodology and it

00:41:20.577 --> 00:41:21.680
and it may also address a question

00:41:21.680 --> 00:41:23.723
that came in through the Q&amp;A.

00:41:23.723 --> 00:41:26.520
You know, you worked on
a collaborative project

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:30.000
that was also a project that
was unfolding in realtime,

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.340
and we of course saw this
last night in the debate,

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:34.950
these issues foregrounded centrally

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:36.420
by the current president.

00:41:36.420 --> 00:41:37.630
I'd love to hear you talk a little bit

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:39.370
about the methodology that you used

00:41:39.370 --> 00:41:42.788
with your co-author and how
you approached this project,

00:41:42.788 --> 00:41:45.610
studying things that really
were unfolding around you

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:47.750
even as you were trying
to make sense of them.

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:49.950
- Yeah, thank you,
Steve, for that question.

00:41:51.180 --> 00:41:54.960
Just on the collaborative
part, I was working on this

00:41:54.960 --> 00:41:57.390
at the same time as
several edited volumes,

00:41:57.390 --> 00:42:00.860
and I've just, I'm back to
writing something my own now

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:03.663
and I just found collaborative
writing to be so,

00:42:04.542 --> 00:42:06.530
it kind of just opened
up so many possibilities

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:09.262
and I've just thought a
little bit more about why.

00:42:09.262 --> 00:42:10.900
And part is there's just
a built-in accountability

00:42:10.900 --> 00:42:12.786
when you're writing together

00:42:12.786 --> 00:42:14.800
to be constantly engaged with one another.

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:15.900
There's something also about it,

00:42:15.900 --> 00:42:17.130
the way it treats writing,

00:42:17.130 --> 00:42:18.980
it's always an open-ended practice.

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:20.580
So you can write something, share it,

00:42:20.580 --> 00:42:22.770
get feedback, add something.

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:24.560
So the sense that everything you produce

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:27.145
has to be finished and
complete tends to diminish.

00:42:27.145 --> 00:42:29.960
And there's just an active
way in which thinking

00:42:29.960 --> 00:42:32.830
and writing together when
you're in conversation

00:42:32.830 --> 00:42:35.020
just really for me opened
up a writing process.

00:42:35.020 --> 00:42:37.020
So we would often do this back and forth

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:40.470
because my coauthor was in
Oregon and I was in Connecticut,

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:43.630
through texts, through other short emails.

00:42:43.630 --> 00:42:45.360
But I realized that was
part of the thinking,

00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:48.470
these very short efforts
to try to offer a take.

00:42:48.470 --> 00:42:50.090
And it just speaks to the second question.

00:42:50.090 --> 00:42:53.023
I mean, we were trying
to, at different points,

00:42:53.023 --> 00:42:55.271
write about the militia
occupation that was happening

00:42:55.271 --> 00:42:56.640
in Eastern Oregon and late 2016,

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:59.170
all of these kind of,
some of the conflicts

00:42:59.170 --> 00:43:00.530
that were happening in Portland.

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:03.840
So in that sense, these very
kind of shorter exchanges

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:06.627
in which we were trying to just
get our heads around things

00:43:06.627 --> 00:43:09.266
really was a certain kind
of writing methodology.

00:43:09.266 --> 00:43:12.060
And the last thing I'll
just say about the chaos is,

00:43:12.060 --> 00:43:13.940
I mean, I so appreciate
what Laura is saying

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:17.170
about this effort to get things right,

00:43:17.170 --> 00:43:19.010
to feel like you're
accurately representing,

00:43:19.010 --> 00:43:20.760
you're spending enough time where you can,

00:43:20.760 --> 00:43:22.880
you're not just leaping to a conclusion.

00:43:22.880 --> 00:43:25.290
For us, I think we were
relieved of some of that,

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:26.440
because as things were happening,

00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:27.520
there were other people writing

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:30.450
in much more comprehensive
and longer-term ways

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:31.480
based on interviews.

00:43:31.480 --> 00:43:34.270
We wanted to try to think
about certain kinds of like,

00:43:34.270 --> 00:43:36.871
just takes, impressions when we were

00:43:36.871 --> 00:43:38.560
putting things together.

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:41.237
So for me, at least I felt a
little free from some of that,

00:43:41.237 --> 00:43:42.900
but there would be other scholars

00:43:42.900 --> 00:43:44.490
really trying to offer the kind

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:47.100
of definitive archival
account of these episodes,

00:43:47.100 --> 00:43:48.590
and we could focus in some ways

00:43:48.590 --> 00:43:50.940
on the politics of them
and their implications.

00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:54.171
- Great, thank you.

00:43:54.171 --> 00:43:55.810
Ana, let me turn to you.

00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:58.008
We have a question in the chat

00:43:58.008 --> 00:43:59.440
about publishing one's first book

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:01.020
and then publishing a second book

00:44:01.020 --> 00:44:03.350
and what you might say to yourself now,

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:05.870
back then when you were
working on your first book,

00:44:05.870 --> 00:44:09.221
having since then had some
experience with publishing.

00:44:09.221 --> 00:44:11.080
Could you talk about
some of the similarities

00:44:11.080 --> 00:44:14.060
and differences between working
on your first pot project

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:15.920
and seeing it through to
publication and working

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:18.570
on this project and seeing
it through to publication?

00:44:19.900 --> 00:44:21.990
Yes, that's a great question.

00:44:21.990 --> 00:44:23.270
And it makes me reflect

00:44:23.270 --> 00:44:25.640
on two moments of my life that
were completely different.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:27.440
I mean, and I think that the book

00:44:27.440 --> 00:44:30.420
and the publication is very much informed

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:33.410
by what predates that process, right,

00:44:33.410 --> 00:44:36.890
like where you are in
your life in that moment.

00:44:36.890 --> 00:44:39.457
My first book, which was
in Puerto Rican Chicago,

00:44:39.457 --> 00:44:44.457
the experience of doing field
work among the activists

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:46.740
in Puerto Rican Chicago changed my life

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:49.550
and shaped who I became as a human being.

00:44:49.550 --> 00:44:51.240
That's not the same thing,

00:44:51.240 --> 00:44:54.120
and that is reflected in the
kind of writing that I did.

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:57.949
It was very difficult
for me to remain critical

00:44:57.949 --> 00:44:59.890
of anything that I was looking at.

00:44:59.890 --> 00:45:04.890
I mean, I had like a stronger
sense to please an audience.

00:45:05.330 --> 00:45:08.010
I felt that it was very
tough to write that book

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:10.620
from a very emotional perspective,

00:45:10.620 --> 00:45:12.210
and I didn't know how books work.

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:14.580
I mean, I was one of these
people that I just like

00:45:14.580 --> 00:45:16.170
had no idea what do I do?

00:45:16.170 --> 00:45:18.100
So I just started calling presses.

00:45:18.100 --> 00:45:20.040
It's like, Oh, well, just call
the University of Chicago,

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:21.290
I'll call it this other one.

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:23.510
And it turned out that, I mean,

00:45:23.510 --> 00:45:25.050
one of those lucky moments,

00:45:25.050 --> 00:45:27.360
like I called the University of Chicago

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:31.300
and the secretary to the
editor of anthropology

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:33.783
and sociology at the time, David Brent,

00:45:33.783 --> 00:45:35.380
just picked up and he's
like, Oh, he's here.

00:45:35.380 --> 00:45:38.010
So I just pitched the
thing in two sentences.

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:39.337
He's like, Oh, yeah, and he's like,

00:45:39.337 --> 00:45:43.207
Yeah, send me the whole thing.

00:45:43.207 --> 00:45:44.760
So that freaked me out.

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:46.040
It's like, Oh my God,
you're not going to ask

00:45:46.040 --> 00:45:46.920
for a proposal?

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:48.010
Send me the whole thing.

00:45:48.010 --> 00:45:49.810
So the whole thing wasn't ready obviously

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:51.680
because why would it, you know,

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:52.980
I'm just checking and calling.

00:45:52.980 --> 00:45:56.410
So I just tried to put it
together and just send it.

00:45:56.410 --> 00:45:59.710
I feel that, and that was a
wonderful first experience.

00:45:59.710 --> 00:46:02.387
I think it is very rare that
things happen that way now.

00:46:02.387 --> 00:46:07.097
This is back in 2000,
I think, around there.

00:46:07.097 --> 00:46:11.853
And so for the last book, I felt it was,

00:46:12.930 --> 00:46:15.540
my interest in the book was academic

00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:19.530
and it was out of certain
curiosity about having become

00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:22.880
a mother at the time and
feeling completely overwhelmed,

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.843
not by raising a child,
but by the social pressures

00:46:25.843 --> 00:46:27.730
of other parents.

00:46:27.730 --> 00:46:29.988
So it became being in this hell

00:46:29.988 --> 00:46:32.080
of like New York City parenting.

00:46:32.080 --> 00:46:34.520
And I said, you know, first, I
need to get out of this hell.

00:46:34.520 --> 00:46:36.494
So let's get out of New York and the US

00:46:36.494 --> 00:46:38.077
(speaking faintly)

00:46:38.077 --> 00:46:40.520
And secondly, you know, I
couldn't just get out of the hell.

00:46:40.520 --> 00:46:42.550
I needed to bring my
child with me to the hell

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:44.650
because I can't, you
know, I have a child now.

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:47.650
And so this became something that began

00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:50.750
in terms of trying to
understand that reality

00:46:50.750 --> 00:46:52.890
and the pressure and the awfulness,

00:46:52.890 --> 00:46:56.273
like genuinely becoming an
awful person at moments because

00:46:56.273 --> 00:46:58.090
of this role that I was filling

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:01.159
and understanding that
that these role is not

00:47:01.159 --> 00:47:02.440
about my own psychological existence.

00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:05.470
There is this very,
very structural element

00:47:05.470 --> 00:47:10.120
to what parenting looks like
and trying to understand that.

00:47:10.120 --> 00:47:11.530
So by the time that I was writing,

00:47:11.530 --> 00:47:12.670
that it was coming together,

00:47:12.670 --> 00:47:15.030
that I was coming up with the book,

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:18.490
what really sort of was in my head most

00:47:18.490 --> 00:47:23.490
is like how much these
parents reassemble my friends,

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:25.425
myself and my friends oftentimes.

00:47:25.425 --> 00:47:28.470
And so is coming to terms with that,

00:47:28.470 --> 00:47:30.770
with that contradiction of, you know,

00:47:30.770 --> 00:47:34.020
I'm critical of these people,
but we are all part of this,

00:47:34.020 --> 00:47:35.500
and we are all staying in this.

00:47:35.500 --> 00:47:36.830
How do we dismantle it?

00:47:36.830 --> 00:47:41.530
Because obviously all this is
doing is enhancing inequality

00:47:41.530 --> 00:47:45.120
even if it's unintentional or whatever.

00:47:45.120 --> 00:47:49.150
And so for that, I also
got very lucky with this,

00:47:49.150 --> 00:47:51.570
but I did have a book proposal ready

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:53.670
just because I needed
to organize my thoughts.

00:47:53.670 --> 00:47:55.610
You know, once you come
back from fieldwork,

00:47:55.610 --> 00:47:58.670
you have 20 million, you know,

00:47:58.670 --> 00:48:02.110
like all these transplant
interviews to transcribe,

00:48:02.110 --> 00:48:04.240
fieldwork field notes, you have like tons

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:06.110
and tons and tons of documents and a way

00:48:06.110 --> 00:48:08.100
for me to get it together a little bit

00:48:08.100 --> 00:48:10.613
was to organize it in
a book proposal format,

00:48:10.613 --> 00:48:13.160
and then I just reached out to Duke

00:48:13.160 --> 00:48:15.640
because I had liked some
work that they had done

00:48:15.640 --> 00:48:18.970
and I touched base with Gisela Fosado,

00:48:18.970 --> 00:48:21.190
the Chicana amazing editor,

00:48:21.190 --> 00:48:23.290
and it was a joy from then on.

00:48:23.290 --> 00:48:25.990
But yeah, but the two were very different.

00:48:25.990 --> 00:48:27.603
And I appreciate that question.

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:30.560
- Thanks, Ana.

00:48:30.560 --> 00:48:33.206
So you've talked a little
bit about the reception

00:48:33.206 --> 00:48:37.400
of your work by editors,
both at Chicago and at Duke.

00:48:37.400 --> 00:48:39.440
And Dan, I know you need
to leave a little earlier

00:48:39.440 --> 00:48:40.280
than the other panelists,

00:48:40.280 --> 00:48:41.600
so I want to ask you a question

00:48:41.600 --> 00:48:43.500
about the reception of your work.

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:44.780
You can take this in a direction

00:48:44.780 --> 00:48:46.870
that talks about how you
worked with the press,

00:48:46.870 --> 00:48:48.110
if you'd like to.

00:48:48.110 --> 00:48:50.190
But I actually wanted to
ask you more generally

00:48:50.190 --> 00:48:54.400
about how you see the book
on doing work in the world

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:57.100
since its publication,
who you think has read it,

00:48:57.100 --> 00:48:58.860
if you've gotten any feedback from it,

00:48:58.860 --> 00:49:00.330
who you'd like to see read it,

00:49:00.330 --> 00:49:02.530
and what you'd like to see
as the impact of the book.

00:49:02.530 --> 00:49:04.943
- Yeah, thank you,
Steve, for that question.

00:49:04.943 --> 00:49:06.890
You know, for me, the most
gratifying ways to connect

00:49:06.890 --> 00:49:10.213
to readers have been talks
we've been able to give

00:49:10.213 --> 00:49:14.460
with some labor unions,
community-based organizations

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:17.013
at other sites which has
involved what, you know,

00:49:17.013 --> 00:49:19.330
I think is the rich work
of trying to figure out

00:49:19.330 --> 00:49:22.150
the relevance of the
material and its insights

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:24.880
to people's active organizing,

00:49:24.880 --> 00:49:27.180
understandings of the
political landscape, et cetera.

00:49:27.180 --> 00:49:29.870
So those have been the most satisfying.

00:49:29.870 --> 00:49:32.690
I think that more challenging
part, there's been two things.

00:49:32.690 --> 00:49:35.440
One is to write a book that
feels like rigorous enough

00:49:35.440 --> 00:49:37.590
for our colleagues,
our academic colleagues

00:49:38.713 --> 00:49:40.730
that draws on literature,
relevant literature,

00:49:40.730 --> 00:49:42.847
while making it accessible
to a wider audience.

00:49:42.847 --> 00:49:45.290
And I have to say, that's difficult to do

00:49:45.290 --> 00:49:46.840
and something we struggled with,

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:48.223
and I still struggle with.

00:49:49.390 --> 00:49:51.840
The other part, I think,
has been like how to not

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:56.040
just turn these into, a lot
of the interviews we did,

00:49:56.040 --> 00:49:57.490
they were kind of like, can you talk to us

00:49:57.490 --> 00:49:59.350
about these odd characters

00:49:59.350 --> 00:50:01.810
and offer some insight
into their psychology,

00:50:01.810 --> 00:50:03.400
which was not the point of the book.

00:50:03.400 --> 00:50:05.200
And part of what I realized is that,

00:50:05.200 --> 00:50:07.700
kind of taken out of context,

00:50:07.700 --> 00:50:09.200
that's part of what people can think

00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:10.410
that the work was about.

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:13.780
So just kind of like funny people

00:50:13.780 --> 00:50:16.840
who have lost their orientation.

00:50:16.840 --> 00:50:18.936
So I guess, I'm saying that's, for us,

00:50:18.936 --> 00:50:21.570
been part of a balance between, you know,

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:23.830
trying to remain speaks
well to some of what we hope

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:26.187
is like the rigor in the
book and the analysis,

00:50:26.187 --> 00:50:28.710
and that it's pushing
while also having it feel

00:50:28.710 --> 00:50:32.550
like it's accessible in places
and that it's been easier.

00:50:32.550 --> 00:50:35.175
So we're in the same room
talking to people about it,

00:50:35.175 --> 00:50:37.890
listening from them,
getting ideas from them,

00:50:37.890 --> 00:50:40.890
and that's also been the most
satisfying part of it as well.

00:50:42.730 --> 00:50:44.000
- Great, thank you.

00:50:44.000 --> 00:50:47.460
Laura, let me ask you then a
version of that same question,

00:50:47.460 --> 00:50:51.270
just, you know, how you see
your book working in the world,

00:50:51.270 --> 00:50:54.710
if it's had a life since
it was published last year,

00:50:54.710 --> 00:50:57.330
if you have any sense about
how it's been received

00:50:57.330 --> 00:50:58.480
since it was published.

00:50:59.972 --> 00:51:01.920
- I do, thank you for asking.

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:04.790
I think one of the things that
I learned from my first book,

00:51:04.790 --> 00:51:07.550
to answer the previous
question that I didn't expect,

00:51:07.550 --> 00:51:10.600
but I was able to expect it
the second time around is that,

00:51:10.600 --> 00:51:12.550
as authors, you know,
we work on these things

00:51:12.550 --> 00:51:14.240
for many years and then we send them out

00:51:14.240 --> 00:51:16.690
in the world and there's a
feeling that we are done.

00:51:16.690 --> 00:51:18.620
But in fact, that is the first time

00:51:18.620 --> 00:51:20.370
that the rest of the world is seeing it,

00:51:20.370 --> 00:51:22.980
and so that's actually in
many ways like the beginning

00:51:22.980 --> 00:51:26.030
of the book's life, and that
can be difficult actually.

00:51:26.030 --> 00:51:29.240
Like I turned this book
in and I was exhausted

00:51:29.240 --> 00:51:30.800
and did not want to think
about it for awhile,

00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:33.550
and then, you know, the press
requests start rolling in.

00:51:33.550 --> 00:51:36.530
So that is something to
think about for those

00:51:36.530 --> 00:51:39.190
of you who are publishing your first book,

00:51:39.190 --> 00:51:40.960
or really any book.

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:44.670
For me, some of the, I
think of a few things.

00:51:44.670 --> 00:51:48.590
One is that I was able
to include photographs

00:51:48.590 --> 00:51:51.870
by a really incredible San
Antonio-based photographer,

00:51:51.870 --> 00:51:55.367
Al Rendon, as he graciously
gave me permission

00:51:55.367 --> 00:51:58.220
to use images on the cover of the book,

00:51:58.220 --> 00:52:01.180
and I have in the book,
this photographic interlude,

00:52:01.180 --> 00:52:03.340
mostly with photos taken by him,

00:52:03.340 --> 00:52:06.550
that tries to just explain
what the different events

00:52:06.550 --> 00:52:08.500
are in the charreria.

00:52:08.500 --> 00:52:11.310
And for me, one of the things
that was most gratifying

00:52:11.310 --> 00:52:14.010
when the book was published
was to see him promote it

00:52:14.010 --> 00:52:16.360
on Facebook because of
how meaningful it was

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:19.170
for him as a photographer who had spent

00:52:19.170 --> 00:52:21.920
like a decade of his life
photographing these communities.

00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:23.880
And so I hadn't really intended that,

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:25.580
but to see the ways that he,

00:52:25.580 --> 00:52:27.020
and then I later started to hear

00:52:27.020 --> 00:52:30.029
about so many people who
learned about their families

00:52:30.029 --> 00:52:33.000
through these, learned
about their communities,

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:34.600
saw their communities in this book,

00:52:34.600 --> 00:52:36.640
and that was really gratifying.

00:52:36.640 --> 00:52:39.530
As one example which is
probably my favorite story

00:52:39.530 --> 00:52:43.000
to share about this, after
I, maybe like six months ago,

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:44.800
I got an email from a Chicano woman

00:52:44.800 --> 00:52:47.870
in San Diego who told me
that she found my book

00:52:47.870 --> 00:52:50.406
on Amazon because, she's not an academic.

00:52:50.406 --> 00:52:53.120
She found my book on Amazon

00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:56.440
because she was going to
redecorate her living room

00:52:56.440 --> 00:52:59.340
in charro and ranch style decor

00:52:59.340 --> 00:53:02.420
and was searching on Amazon for charros,

00:53:02.420 --> 00:53:03.990
and there popped up my book.

00:53:03.990 --> 00:53:06.290
And she, so that alone is amazing, right,

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:08.460
that people might find our
work when they're not looking

00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:09.910
for anything academic.

00:53:09.910 --> 00:53:11.710
But she bought it and she read it,

00:53:11.710 --> 00:53:15.670
and in the very first
chapter, she saw her father,

00:53:15.670 --> 00:53:18.010
she read about her father and her uncle

00:53:18.010 --> 00:53:19.320
and some of her neighbors.

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:22.020
And then she wrote to me
and she was like, Oh my God,

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:23.240
you know, like I can't believe it.

00:53:23.240 --> 00:53:26.360
And then she sent me
photos that I, you know,

00:53:26.360 --> 00:53:28.860
and it was, because I didn't
do a lot of interviews

00:53:28.860 --> 00:53:31.396
and I didn't do ethnography for this book,

00:53:31.396 --> 00:53:35.290
it was just like to hear that
the book could be useful,

00:53:35.290 --> 00:53:36.670
that it could connect with the people

00:53:36.670 --> 00:53:38.360
who I was trying to represent

00:53:38.360 --> 00:53:40.943
using other kinds of sources was super,

00:53:43.093 --> 00:53:45.270
it just made me glad that I had done it,

00:53:45.270 --> 00:53:46.740
you know, even though
the book was challenging

00:53:46.740 --> 00:53:47.573
in so many ways.

00:53:47.573 --> 00:53:49.410
And then one last thing
that I'll add is that

00:53:49.410 --> 00:53:52.740
I am now copied on virtually every email

00:53:52.740 --> 00:53:55.590
that charros send to their legislators.

00:53:55.590 --> 00:53:58.890
You know, I'm in a long list
of BCCs or CCs, you know,

00:53:58.890 --> 00:54:01.160
and so I think that they
see me as at least somebody

00:54:01.160 --> 00:54:02.720
who is following and
tracking what they do,

00:54:02.720 --> 00:54:03.880
which I am.

00:54:03.880 --> 00:54:05.620
And it's just been super interesting.

00:54:05.620 --> 00:54:07.390
None of those things are
things I would have predicted

00:54:07.390 --> 00:54:10.700
and they go well beyond
the academic circles

00:54:10.700 --> 00:54:13.023
that I was probably writing for primarily.

00:54:15.140 --> 00:54:17.490
- Ana, I know you've you wrote your book

00:54:17.490 --> 00:54:21.235
I think it's fair to say
primarily for academic audiences,

00:54:21.235 --> 00:54:24.110
but I wonder if you also
had your own interlocutors

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:25.580
in mind as potential readers

00:54:25.580 --> 00:54:28.680
and if you could talk about how
you see your book being read

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:32.120
and what kinds of responses
you've received thus far.

00:54:32.120 --> 00:54:34.460
- Yes, I'm thinking when
I was listening to Laura,

00:54:34.460 --> 00:54:35.860
I'm thinking of my first book that,

00:54:35.860 --> 00:54:37.580
the book of Puerto Rican Chicago,

00:54:37.580 --> 00:54:41.110
that became a study book
for the very activist

00:54:41.110 --> 00:54:43.130
which were part of the book.

00:54:43.130 --> 00:54:44.590
So they would meet a book clubs.

00:54:44.590 --> 00:54:48.330
I mean, these were very, a
group that was very much aware

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:51.520
of how history mattered in the present.

00:54:51.520 --> 00:54:55.566
And so, I think that
was for me very unusual.

00:54:55.566 --> 00:54:58.590
I mean, the fact that the very
people who were on the book

00:54:58.590 --> 00:54:59.750
are reading it.

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:01.130
And of course, they
could tell who was what,

00:55:01.130 --> 00:55:03.260
and that caused all sorts of other things,

00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:07.392
even though I had tried to
conceal a lot of, you know?

00:55:07.392 --> 00:55:11.170
This current book came out
on the same day that we went

00:55:11.170 --> 00:55:12.640
on lockdown in New York.

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:14.920
So all the different
kinds of presentations,

00:55:14.920 --> 00:55:17.048
all the different kinds
of things that I wanted

00:55:17.048 --> 00:55:19.259
(speaking faintly) to
some people that had been

00:55:19.259 --> 00:55:21.180
in the book and all of
that kind of good stuff.

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:23.940
So the most that I've been able to tell

00:55:23.940 --> 00:55:28.731
in terms of impact, well, impact
in academic settings, yes,

00:55:28.731 --> 00:55:32.630
I mean, I've gotten, you
know, mostly in terms

00:55:32.630 --> 00:55:37.630
of visits to pretty much, I
mean, you know, India, UK,

00:55:39.234 --> 00:55:41.290
I mean, just like a whole bunch of places,

00:55:41.290 --> 00:55:43.270
which surprised me.

00:55:43.270 --> 00:55:44.620
But those are academic settings.

00:55:44.620 --> 00:55:47.594
But in terms of how
it's actually impacted,

00:55:47.594 --> 00:55:50.710
like, I think more of the
people that were in the book

00:55:50.710 --> 00:55:52.180
because this is an ethnographic book,

00:55:52.180 --> 00:55:53.850
so you know, you always wonder,

00:55:53.850 --> 00:55:57.270
and I know that the people
in this book specifically

00:55:57.270 --> 00:55:59.013
will read the book.

00:55:59.013 --> 00:56:01.780
And one of them has already read the book

00:56:01.780 --> 00:56:05.460
and she has been texting me
about wanting to learn more

00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:07.570
about her own white privilege,
which is very interesting.

00:56:07.570 --> 00:56:08.880
I'm just like, wow.

00:56:08.880 --> 00:56:10.743
But, you know, and I told her,

00:56:10.743 --> 00:56:13.240
when I go to Puerto Rico,
we can just get together

00:56:13.240 --> 00:56:16.343
and talk about this because
right now we're on WhatsApp

00:56:16.343 --> 00:56:18.305
and that's like a lot of texting.

00:56:18.305 --> 00:56:21.643
You know, but it was
really interesting to hear

00:56:21.643 --> 00:56:25.277
her reflection on it and
like what she got out of it.

00:56:25.277 --> 00:56:28.140
At that point, she was halfway through,

00:56:28.140 --> 00:56:30.670
so I haven't heard from
her since she finished.

00:56:30.670 --> 00:56:32.760
I don't know if I said anything
like more objectionable

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:35.024
in the last part of it.

00:56:35.024 --> 00:56:39.401
And then actually, another
person from Brazil who works,

00:56:39.401 --> 00:56:43.180
you know, who was very
always been very interested

00:56:43.180 --> 00:56:46.155
in just experts and stuff like that

00:56:46.155 --> 00:56:50.428
also wanted me to give a talk
for one of her organizations.

00:56:50.428 --> 00:56:53.750
So there have been initiatives
like that at this point

00:56:53.750 --> 00:56:57.263
and all of that has been
sort of colored by COVID.

00:56:57.263 --> 00:57:00.560
But yeah, it's always like,
especially for, you know,

00:57:00.560 --> 00:57:02.290
for ethnography, it's
just very interesting

00:57:02.290 --> 00:57:03.760
to see what their reaction is,

00:57:03.760 --> 00:57:08.010
not only for the broader
academic population,

00:57:08.010 --> 00:57:11.890
but also for the people that
feel that they're in the book.

00:57:11.890 --> 00:57:12.940
So, yeah.

00:57:12.940 --> 00:57:13.773
- Great, thank you.

00:57:13.773 --> 00:57:15.720
Let me ask all three
of you a final question

00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:19.780
and you can take this
in whatever way you want

00:57:19.780 --> 00:57:21.580
and tailor it to your own project.

00:57:21.580 --> 00:57:23.980
I mean, it strikes me
that for each of you,

00:57:23.980 --> 00:57:26.178
the project that you're discussing was,

00:57:26.178 --> 00:57:30.410
in some ways, familiar to you in advance

00:57:30.410 --> 00:57:32.780
whether it connected
with personal interests

00:57:32.780 --> 00:57:34.720
and personal experiences, or in some way,

00:57:34.720 --> 00:57:37.840
had relationships to pass
projects that you had done.

00:57:37.840 --> 00:57:40.200
But there are other ways in
which each of your projects,

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:42.301
you know, represented
something significantly new.

00:57:42.301 --> 00:57:46.470
And, you know, I'm thinking
both for Dan and for Ana,

00:57:46.470 --> 00:57:48.870
working with populations, I
think, that you had not worked

00:57:48.870 --> 00:57:51.345
in quite this way academically before.

00:57:51.345 --> 00:57:53.880
So I'd love to hear all three
of you talk a little bit

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:56.780
about how you oriented
yourself to this new material,

00:57:56.780 --> 00:57:59.357
how you thought about your
responsibilities to representing,

00:57:59.357 --> 00:58:03.690
the actors and protagonists
and people you were discussing

00:58:03.690 --> 00:58:05.860
in this work, and what
you felt to be the kind

00:58:05.860 --> 00:58:08.160
of key challenges that you'd
like to share with other people

00:58:08.160 --> 00:58:11.020
in taking on a project
that was in some ways new

00:58:11.020 --> 00:58:12.840
and in which you may have, you know,

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:15.038
been working with with groups of people

00:58:15.038 --> 00:58:17.122
with whom you didn't
share all ways of life

00:58:17.122 --> 00:58:18.939
and all ways of thinking.

00:58:18.939 --> 00:58:21.560
So, you know, Dan, since again,

00:58:21.560 --> 00:58:23.390
I know you're going to need
to take off a little earlier,

00:58:23.390 --> 00:58:24.890
maybe we could start with you,

00:58:26.393 --> 00:58:28.440
and then go to Ana and
then finished with Laura.

00:58:28.440 --> 00:58:29.273
- Sure.

00:58:29.273 --> 00:58:31.350
Great, thanks, Steve, for the question.

00:58:31.350 --> 00:58:33.250
I mean, you know, I spent a lot,

00:58:33.250 --> 00:58:34.930
we spent a lot of time for this project.

00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:37.990
We were at militia rallies, you know,

00:58:37.990 --> 00:58:41.656
endless hours listening to
right-wing radio on those sites.

00:58:41.656 --> 00:58:45.324
And I think what I felt like
I was trying to do there was,

00:58:45.324 --> 00:58:48.390
one, really understand
the kind of like logic,

00:58:48.390 --> 00:58:50.950
the commitments, the worldview,

00:58:50.950 --> 00:58:53.540
listening for the openness to it,

00:58:53.540 --> 00:58:56.810
so it's not just that
I already know the kind

00:58:56.810 --> 00:59:00.290
of narratives and positions
that people hold and embrace,

00:59:00.290 --> 00:59:04.474
and certainly without then
underestimating it's violence,

00:59:04.474 --> 00:59:07.460
the potential harm that it had.

00:59:07.460 --> 00:59:10.020
You know, the day we
interviewed Joey Gibson,

00:59:10.020 --> 00:59:12.410
the next day, he was a
part of a very vicious,

00:59:12.410 --> 00:59:13.670
you know, kind of brawl.

00:59:13.670 --> 00:59:16.700
So we were on the one hand
trying to offer, you know,

00:59:16.700 --> 00:59:19.110
a nuanced account of
the conversation we had

00:59:19.110 --> 00:59:21.450
with him while saying
this that's not meant

00:59:21.450 --> 00:59:23.710
to minimize everything he's done.

00:59:23.710 --> 00:59:25.606
And just the last thing
I'll say is that, you know,

00:59:25.606 --> 00:59:27.120
I'm doing a project now on
this right wing radio host,

00:59:27.120 --> 00:59:28.230
Sonnie Johnson.

00:59:28.230 --> 00:59:31.380
There are long periods of
time when I listen to her

00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:36.380
when she's totally criticizing,

00:59:37.760 --> 00:59:41.460
lecturing white conservatives
about their racism.

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:44.530
And I strangely identify with her, like,

00:59:44.530 --> 00:59:47.470
anti-racist commitments
that she's trying to enact

00:59:47.470 --> 00:59:49.970
even though that anti-racism is in service

00:59:49.970 --> 00:59:51.640
of a far different vision.

00:59:51.640 --> 00:59:54.169
So I actually, I don't
seem to then see all

00:59:54.169 --> 00:59:56.768
the connections we can
make as much as just

00:59:56.768 --> 01:00:00.060
as these ideas and possibilities

01:00:00.060 --> 01:00:03.090
are not somehow like
just the sole possession

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:05.780
of people who I connect
with and identify with.

01:00:05.780 --> 01:00:07.510
Those ideas are out there in the world.

01:00:07.510 --> 01:00:09.440
They may have come from our communities

01:00:09.440 --> 01:00:12.010
and intellectual traditions,
but many, many people

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:13.780
are taking them up, and I think that has

01:00:13.780 --> 01:00:15.030
to be taken seriously.

01:00:15.030 --> 01:00:16.877
Otherwise, all you do is
just dismiss it and say,

01:00:16.877 --> 01:00:19.923
you know, you shouldn't even
be having this conversation.

01:00:22.013 --> 01:00:23.783
But that's partly what I've learned here.

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:29.608
- Great, I mean, just listening to that,

01:00:29.608 --> 01:00:33.290
you know, it reminds me of
like all this conversation

01:00:33.290 --> 01:00:36.140
about cultural appropriation
and to what degree

01:00:36.140 --> 01:00:39.110
are these debates becoming
this culturally appropriated,

01:00:39.110 --> 01:00:42.120
like appropriated phenomenon.

01:00:42.120 --> 01:00:45.900
So I think that's, even though, yes,

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:48.480
these populations were
different from what I've used,

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:50.890
what I've done, the kind of
work that I've done before,

01:00:50.890 --> 01:00:52.260
I've always really been interested

01:00:52.260 --> 01:00:55.220
in how psychology operates
in a sociological way.

01:00:55.220 --> 01:00:57.901
I mean, like how therapy, psychology,

01:00:57.901 --> 01:01:02.830
affect even acquire a material life,

01:01:02.830 --> 01:01:06.319
and I think that that
continues into this project.

01:01:06.319 --> 01:01:07.870
In that sense, it's not super different

01:01:07.870 --> 01:01:10.550
from that other kind of project.

01:01:10.550 --> 01:01:12.650
What was different, and
it was very helpful,

01:01:12.650 --> 01:01:15.141
is to understand how Latinx studies

01:01:15.141 --> 01:01:20.141
and Latin-American studies
need to be examine in junction.

01:01:20.170 --> 01:01:21.830
They need to be examined together.

01:01:21.830 --> 01:01:24.680
I mean, there is so
much wealth of materials

01:01:24.680 --> 01:01:28.743
on both sides that will benefit
our reading of the other.

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:32.515
So that actually led to the next,

01:01:32.515 --> 01:01:34.819
the edited volume that Steve mentioned,

01:01:34.819 --> 01:01:36.595
(speaking faintly)

01:01:36.595 --> 01:01:40.160
that I'm doing with my
colleague and friend, Joshua.

01:01:40.160 --> 01:01:43.590
So one of the things that I felt

01:01:43.590 --> 01:01:47.115
that came out of that process
of the book, you know,

01:01:47.115 --> 01:01:52.115
that hadn't come from
previous work was the clarity

01:01:53.010 --> 01:01:56.670
of those connections and
those points of convergence

01:01:56.670 --> 01:01:57.883
between the fields.

01:01:59.256 --> 01:02:02.110
So I would say that
that had been something

01:02:02.110 --> 01:02:05.253
that I will continue to think
through and try to implement,

01:02:05.253 --> 01:02:07.310
not only in my research,
but also in my teaching

01:02:07.310 --> 01:02:10.340
and the classes that I put together.

01:02:10.340 --> 01:02:15.215
Because I am very interested
in making sure that,

01:02:15.215 --> 01:02:17.160
in classroom discussions,

01:02:17.160 --> 01:02:22.160
we have the grasp of a
broader body of literature

01:02:22.330 --> 01:02:24.460
even when the grasp is Latinx studies

01:02:24.460 --> 01:02:25.793
or Latin American studies.

01:02:30.328 --> 01:02:33.500
- For me, this question about how do we,

01:02:33.500 --> 01:02:35.500
I hear it partly as how
do we study communities

01:02:35.500 --> 01:02:37.210
that we're not fully members of?

01:02:37.210 --> 01:02:40.320
And that was a complicated thing for me

01:02:40.320 --> 01:02:42.640
that mostly came down to
the methods that I chose

01:02:42.640 --> 01:02:43.473
for this book.

01:02:43.473 --> 01:02:46.520
So my first book I had
done deep hanging out

01:02:46.520 --> 01:02:48.020
of the kind that Ana is talking about.

01:02:48.020 --> 01:02:49.950
I wrote about the community
that I grew up in.

01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:52.170
I went to four years of really difficult

01:02:52.170 --> 01:02:54.909
and painful homeowner's
association meetings.

01:02:54.909 --> 01:02:56.710
I was writing about white
folks that I grew up with,

01:02:56.710 --> 01:02:59.830
mostly white women and the way
that they thought about land.

01:02:59.830 --> 01:03:02.910
And in that sense, that
was a very personal book.

01:03:02.910 --> 01:03:04.888
So for the second book, though,

01:03:04.888 --> 01:03:08.420
I was writing about people who
lived in nearby neighborhoods

01:03:08.420 --> 01:03:11.870
but were men, were older
men, were Mexican-American

01:03:11.870 --> 01:03:13.241
or Mexican immigrant.

01:03:13.241 --> 01:03:17.460
And so I had to think really
hard, methodologically,

01:03:17.460 --> 01:03:20.161
how could I offer something useful?

01:03:20.161 --> 01:03:22.780
And it became clear to me pretty quickly

01:03:22.780 --> 01:03:26.300
that I could not and did
not want to do ethnography.

01:03:26.300 --> 01:03:28.820
And that was hard for
me for a little while

01:03:28.820 --> 01:03:30.940
to kind of wrap my head
around because I had been

01:03:30.940 --> 01:03:34.069
so committed to ethnography
in the first book.

01:03:34.069 --> 01:03:36.240
I think it was also
important to say, you know,

01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:37.890
there were logistical things here

01:03:37.890 --> 01:03:40.352
that any of us has to think about.

01:03:40.352 --> 01:03:42.080
I moved to the east coast.

01:03:42.080 --> 01:03:44.720
There are some rodeo and
BUH--KAIR-OH scenes here,

01:03:44.720 --> 01:03:47.084
but they're not charros
and it's not the place

01:03:47.084 --> 01:03:49.302
that I was trying to write about.

01:03:49.302 --> 01:03:52.640
So logistically it became really difficult

01:03:52.640 --> 01:03:54.860
for me to imagine doing deep ethnography

01:03:54.860 --> 01:03:56.240
with the folks that I was writing about.

01:03:56.240 --> 01:03:59.533
But also there had been, and there still

01:03:59.533 --> 01:04:03.240
are pretty active animal welfare campaigns

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:05.560
directed at charros and
they are led by people

01:04:05.560 --> 01:04:08.490
who look like me, white
women, middle-aged,

01:04:08.490 --> 01:04:09.600
who grew up with animals.

01:04:09.600 --> 01:04:13.381
And so it was actually
difficult for me to gain access.

01:04:13.381 --> 01:04:16.365
And I worry that if I had
chosen to do a different

01:04:16.365 --> 01:04:19.450
kind of method, that so
much of the work would

01:04:19.450 --> 01:04:22.530
have been charros trying to
convince me to be their ally

01:04:22.530 --> 01:04:24.630
and their advocate in
animal welfare debates.

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:26.550
And the two interactions that I did have

01:04:26.550 --> 01:04:29.137
that were interviews, longer interviews,

01:04:29.137 --> 01:04:31.590
both took that direction really quickly.

01:04:31.590 --> 01:04:33.370
And so I had to step back from that

01:04:33.370 --> 01:04:35.971
and figure out a different
method that could

01:04:35.971 --> 01:04:37.440
still allow me to answer the questions

01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:38.850
that I was curious about,

01:04:38.850 --> 01:04:42.420
but also that could be
useful to the scholarship.

01:04:42.420 --> 01:04:44.620
And so I really thought
about a lot of this book

01:04:44.620 --> 01:04:47.260
was writing in service to
and to try to be useful

01:04:47.260 --> 01:04:50.421
to my own mentors who were
mostly in Chicano studies,

01:04:50.421 --> 01:04:53.210
and to try to do something
that could be useful

01:04:53.210 --> 01:04:54.480
to them and to the field.

01:04:54.480 --> 01:04:57.390
And it also became about
writing something useful

01:04:57.390 --> 01:04:59.700
for the communities that
I was writing about,

01:04:59.700 --> 01:05:01.010
but that was not ethnographic.

01:05:01.010 --> 01:05:03.130
And again, that was where
I was able to identify

01:05:03.130 --> 01:05:05.550
that there was no history
of these communities

01:05:05.550 --> 01:05:06.420
in the United States.

01:05:06.420 --> 01:05:07.900
There were a few ethnographies.

01:05:07.900 --> 01:05:11.550
There was no history and there
was no comparative geography.

01:05:11.550 --> 01:05:16.037
And so I landed on that
and I feel okay about that.

01:05:16.037 --> 01:05:18.210
Even though it was such a different thing

01:05:18.210 --> 01:05:20.340
from my first project it became necessary

01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:23.330
because of my own
subjectivity, my own location,

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:25.530
geographically subject
location in relationship

01:05:25.530 --> 01:05:27.040
to the folks I was writing about.

01:05:27.040 --> 01:05:29.180
And I think the larger
lesson that that taught me

01:05:29.180 --> 01:05:31.600
which has become important
as I've matured a bit

01:05:31.600 --> 01:05:33.597
as a scholar is to really acknowledge

01:05:33.597 --> 01:05:37.530
and embrace the fact that we
can use very different methods

01:05:37.530 --> 01:05:40.310
for different projects and
that we can feel differently

01:05:40.310 --> 01:05:41.620
about our projects.

01:05:41.620 --> 01:05:43.800
The first book was very personal.

01:05:43.800 --> 01:05:46.438
The second book was less so,
it was more professional,

01:05:46.438 --> 01:05:50.008
or I pursued it for more
sort of professional

01:05:50.008 --> 01:05:51.470
and political reasons,

01:05:51.470 --> 01:05:54.110
and that in our long lifespan, hopefully,

01:05:54.110 --> 01:05:56.010
we get to work on more than one project.

01:05:56.010 --> 01:05:58.320
But that there's room for all of that

01:05:58.320 --> 01:06:00.050
and that we can do different kinds

01:06:00.050 --> 01:06:01.800
of work at different moments in our life,

01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:04.270
depending on how we're positioned

01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:06.020
and who we're in relationship with.

01:06:08.150 --> 01:06:10.450
- Thank you, Laura, and thank
you, Ana, and thank you, Dan.

01:06:10.450 --> 01:06:12.160
I think that's a great place to close.

01:06:12.160 --> 01:06:14.730
I so appreciate the opportunity to hear

01:06:14.730 --> 01:06:18.396
from all three of you about
how your books were made.

01:06:18.396 --> 01:06:20.810
And again, for our audience members too,

01:06:20.810 --> 01:06:23.504
our audience members,
thank you for coming.

01:06:23.504 --> 01:06:24.590
You can see the titles of these books

01:06:24.590 --> 01:06:27.650
and some more information
about them in the chat,

01:06:27.650 --> 01:06:28.940
if you're interested.

01:06:28.940 --> 01:06:31.590
Laura Barraclough's book is
Charros: How Mexican Cowboys

01:06:31.590 --> 01:06:34.090
are Remapping Race and American Identity.

01:06:34.090 --> 01:06:36.810
Dan HoSang's book is
Producers, Parasites, Patriots:

01:06:36.810 --> 01:06:39.145
Race and the new Right-Wing
Politics of Precarity.

01:06:39.145 --> 01:06:43.900
And Ana Ramos-Zaya's book
is Parenting Empires:

01:06:43.900 --> 01:06:45.740
Class, Whiteness, and the Moral Economy

01:06:45.740 --> 01:06:47.310
of Privilege in Latin America.

01:06:47.310 --> 01:06:49.675
This has been a great conversation.

01:06:49.675 --> 01:06:51.090
I really appreciated hearing
about the contents of the book,

01:06:51.090 --> 01:06:54.020
but perhaps more importantly,
how you envisioned

01:06:54.020 --> 01:06:56.950
these books and how you
pursued these topics

01:06:56.950 --> 01:06:58.793
and how you carried them out,

01:06:58.793 --> 01:07:00.568
and how they were ultimately made.

01:07:00.568 --> 01:07:02.590
So thank you so much
for being with us today,

01:07:02.590 --> 01:07:05.120
panelists and attendees,
and we hope to see you soon

01:07:05.120 --> 01:07:06.540
at an RITM event.

01:07:06.540 --> 01:07:07.373
Take care.

